gofer: one Mappable per file, whatever its names

What: a regular file the gofer serves is mapped through its inode, which every
name (hard link) of the file shares while any is in use, instead of through
the dentry of the name it was opened by. The memmap.Mappable methods
(AddMapping, RemoveMapping, CopyMapping, Translate, InvalidateUnsavable) move
from dentry to inode, whose state they already used alone.

Why: the Sentry keys a futex in a shared file mapping by the mapping's
Mappable. glibc's sem_open creates a POSIX semaphore under a temporary name,
maps it, links it to its own name and unlinks the temporary one, so a process
that opens the semaphore by its name, as Python's multiprocessing does in its
spawn context, mapped it through another dentry than its creator: both saw the
count change, and neither woke the other. Where /dev/shm is a tmpfs on the
host bound into the container, as rootless Podman's --shm-size and Docker's
--ipc=shareable make it, a spawned child's release never woke its parent, and
SGLang-Omni's server never became ready.

Verified on OpenRSI's H100 nodes (Linux 6.8, rootless Podman 4.9.3) and on a
Docker host (Linux 7.0, rootful Docker), /dev/shm a tmpfs on the host bound
into the container by Podman's --shm-size or Docker's --ipc=shareable:
- A process that mapped a file under its name woke, through a futex, one that
  had mapped it under a temporary name since linked to it, in 1.1 s; without
  the patch the waiter was not woken within 5 s, though each saw the other's
  writes.
- A semaphore Python's multiprocessing created in its spawn context woke the
  child and the parent alike, in a container and in a pod; without the patch
  the parent's wait for its child's release ended unanswered after 10 s.
- PyTorch's DataLoader passing 64 images of 3x224x224 float32 at a time
  through /dev/shm ran as fast as without the patch: 4622 to 5260 samples a
  second on one H100 against 4608 to 4946 (gVisor release-20261116.0).
